Z||000030||fyerstarter||04-22-2001||04:36 PM||||Correct me if Im wrong but the only difference between an 88 formula and a Gt of the same year is the wing, nose, tail lights, and fastback clip. So if you switch those parts then it is no longer a Formula and it is now a GT. Not a Formula trying to look like a GT. So there is nothing "rice" about it.||||reg||1
Z||000031||Fiero_RKM||04-22-2001||09:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>...the only difference between an 88 formula and a GT of the same year is the wing, nose, tail lights, and fastback clip. So if you switch those parts then it is no longer a Formula and it is now a GT...<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>True, but the VIN number would still peg it as a Formula.<P>I appreciate all the feedback (much more than I thought I would get).  I decided to just leave it as a stock Formula (especially since it is a pretty nice example).  Maybe I will sell it or trade for a GT especially since I would like to change to an automatic (my arthritic knees are getting bad and clutch is tougher now).<P>Regards...<BR>RKM<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/rkmore_7414/formula.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/rkmore_7414/formula.htm</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000032||ray b||04-22-2001||11:01 PM||||market value will suffer and be less than stock Formula but only to hard core collector. better investment to sell the Formula and buy low miles gt<BR>do it or any mod, only if you will keep the car.not for resale value. ||||reg||1
